\section*{Weak mixing of every triangular billiard with an irrational angle}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
An irrational angle divided by $\pi$ is claimed to force weak mixing for the full billiard flow of any nondegenerate Euclidean triangle, using normalized Liouville measure. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 150, 2026-10-05): Local transport estimates force a possible eigenfunction to be a plane wave with only direction-dependent amplitude. Reflections through the irrational vertex make that amplitude constant, and reflection at the third side excludes any nonzero frequency; the spectral criterion then gives weak mixing.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 150, 2026-10-05): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 150, the scope page documents the following selections. The selection establishes ergodicity and the almost-everywhere flow law for an irrational-angle triangle, including the exceptional-trajectory null set. The weak-mixing companion is not selected. Manuscript 1 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

